IMPORT prelude

-- Local Government Act 1995 (Western Australia), Part 6 Division 6 -- rates and service charges, from the seat of the
-- ratepayer: who levies rates on my home, on what valuation, how much, when they are due and how I may pay, what happens
-- if I pay late, and how I object.
--
-- SEAT: the ratepayer -- the owner of rateable land on which a local government imposes rates ("Who levies rates on my
-- home?"). Every exported question is phrased as that owner would ask it. What the Act tells the local government or the
-- Minister to do (impose, determine, notify, reassess, sell) is encoded only as what it means for the owner: what may be
-- charged, what must be given, and by when.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 6.25 (terms used: owner, rate record, gross rental value, unimproved value); s 6.26(1), (2) (rateable
-- land and the ordinary exemptions); s 6.28 (basis of rates: the Minister's determination, the rural / non-rural general
-- principle, the valuation in force on 1 July or a later interim, amended or new valuation); s 6.32(1), (2) (general rate,
-- specified area rate, minimum payment, service charge; rate in the dollar); s 6.33(3), (4) (differential rates: twice the
-- lowest; no mid-year change for a change of characteristics unless the rateable value changed); s 6.35(1) (minimum payment
-- greater than the general rate); s 6.36(3)(b)(ii) (21 days for submissions on proposed differential rates); s 6.39(2) and
-- s 6.40(1), (3) (amendment of the rate record, reassessment, refund or credit); s 6.41 (rate notice: issue date, email only
-- with consent, deemed service, 28 days before each instalment); s 6.43 (a charge on the land); s 6.44 (owner liable; joint
-- owners jointly and severally); s 6.45 (single payment or 4 instalments; remaining instalments after a reassessment;
-- instalment charge); s 6.46 (discount by resolution; s 6.12(2)); s 6.50 (due date not earlier than 35 days
-- after the notice; instalments at least 2 months apart); s 6.51 (interest on overdue rates; the prescribed maximum; none
-- for a person entitled to a rebate or deferment); s 6.52(1)(a), (2), (3) (apportionment between successive owners); s 6.53
-- (land becoming rateable); s 6.55(1) (from whom recoverable); s 6.56(1) (recovery in court); s 6.60 (a lessee's rent); s
-- 6.61 (naming the owner; $5 000); s 6.62 (order of application of payments); s 6.64(1), (3) (possession after 3 years;
-- caveat); s 6.67(1) (release within 12 years); s 6.69(1), (2) (paying up to 7 days before a sale); ss 6.76-6.78, 6.80-6.82
-- (objection to the rate record, 42 days; State Administrative Tribunal review, 42 days; valuations only under the
-- Valuation of Land Act 1978; liability continues; general review).
--
-- Edition: the Local Government Act 1995 (No. 74 of 1995) as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49599.txt
-- (legislation.wa.gov.au mrdoc 49599, retrieved 2026-09-07); no compilation date stated in the deposit; the Part 6 notes
-- run to No. 7 of 2026 (s 6.26). No regulations are deposited: the Local Government (Financial Management) Regulations
-- 1996 and any other regulations that prescribe the maximum interest rate (s 6.51(2)), the maximum instalment charge
-- (s 6.45(4)(e)), the method of calculating interest (s 6.51(5)) and the minimum payment limits (s 6.35(4)) are NOT
-- deposited: those numbers are INPUTS. s 6.47 (waiver and concessions by resolution) is not encoded. The modifications prescribed by the Local Government (COVID-19 Response) Order 2020
-- (SL 2020/57, note 1M) are not deposited and not applied.
--
-- POINTER, NOT ENCODED HERE: pensioner and seniors rebates and deferments of rates are in the Rates and Charges (Rebates
-- and Deferments) Act 1992 (WA), deposited at ../../../rates-and-charges-rebates-and-deferments-act-1992. This Act connects
-- to it in ss 6.43, 6.46, 6.47, 6.50(1)(c), 6.51(4) and 6.55(1); only s 6.51(4) (no interest and no instalment charge for a
-- person entitled to a rebate or deferment) is encoded, with the entitlement asked as a FACT.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: Part 6 Divisions 1-5 except s 6.12(2) (budgets, funds, fees and charges, borrowing); the mining and petroleum
-- rules (ss 6.26(2A), 6.27, 6.29, 6.30) and the phasing in of valuations (s 6.31, Schedule 6.1); the limits on the yield of
-- the general rate and on minimum payments across the district (ss 6.34, 6.35(2)-(6)); the uses of specified area rates and
-- service charges (ss 6.37, 6.38) beyond their being payable; Subdivision 6 beyond ss 6.64, 6.67, 6.69 (leasing, sale,
-- transfer to the Crown, Schedules 6.2 and 6.3); the valuations themselves (Valuation of Land Act 1978); the emergency
-- services levy and water charges (other Acts).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the seat of the ratepayer, with the design defaults ruled
-- on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice.
-- See NOTES.md.
--
-- Conventions. A day is written as the number YYYYMMDD (15 July 2029 is 20290715). Money is in dollars. A rate in the dollar
-- is a decimal (7 cents in the dollar is 0.07). An interest rate is a percentage a year.

§ `Local Government Act 1995 -- the ratepayer`

§§ `Arithmetic and the calendar`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the greater of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT LEAST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

-- A count of days from a fixed origin, so that two days can be subtracted (the civil-calendar algorithm, Gregorian).
GIVEN `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the day number of` MEANS
    (`era` TIMES 146097) PLUS `doe`
    WHERE
        `y0` MEANS FLOOR (`the day` DIVIDED BY 10000)
        `m` MEANS FLOOR (`the day` DIVIDED BY 100) MINUS (`y0` TIMES 100)
        `d` MEANS `the day` MINUS (FLOOR (`the day` DIVIDED BY 100) TIMES 100)
        `y` MEANS IF `m` AT MOST 2 THEN `y0` MINUS 1 ELSE `y0`
        `era` MEANS FLOOR (`y` DIVIDED BY 400)
        `yoe` MEANS `y` MINUS (`era` TIMES 400)
        `mp` MEANS IF `m` GREATER THAN 2 THEN `m` MINUS 3 ELSE `m` PLUS 9
        `doy` MEANS FLOOR (((153 TIMES `mp`) PLUS 2) DIVIDED BY 5) PLUS `d` MINUS 1
        `doe` MEANS (`yoe` TIMES 365) PLUS FLOOR (`yoe` DIVIDED BY 4) MINUS FLOOR (`yoe` DIVIDED BY 100) PLUS `doy`

GIVEN `the first day` IS A NUMBER, `the second day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days from` MEANS `the day number of` `the second day` MINUS `the day number of` `the first day`

-- The same day of the month two months later (a 31st may land on a day the month lacks; compared as a number it still
-- falls before the first day of the following month, which is the effect intended).
GIVEN `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`two months after` MEANS
    IF `m` AT LEAST 11
    THEN ((`y` PLUS 1) TIMES 10000) PLUS ((`m` MINUS 10) TIMES 100) PLUS `d`
    ELSE `the day` PLUS 200
    WHERE
        `y` MEANS FLOOR (`the day` DIVIDED BY 10000)
        `m` MEANS FLOOR (`the day` DIVIDED BY 100) MINUS (`y` TIMES 100)
        `d` MEANS `the day` MINUS (FLOOR (`the day` DIVIDED BY 100) TIMES 100)

§§ `s 6.26 -- is my land rateable?`

-- s 6.26(1): "Except as provided in this section all land within a district is rateable land."
-- (2) "The following land is not rateable land -- (a) land which is the property of the Crown and -- (i) is being used or
-- held for a public purpose; or (ii) is unoccupied; ... (d) land used or held exclusively by a religious body as a place of
-- public worship ... a place of residence of a minister of religion ...; (f) land used exclusively as a nongovernment
-- school ...; (g) land used exclusively for charitable purposes; ... (j) land which is exempt from rates under any other
-- written law; and (k) land which is declared by the Minister to be exempt from rates."
-- (6) occasional use for another charitable, benevolent, religious or public purpose does not end exclusive use.
-- (b), (c), (e), (h), (i) (local government land, show grounds, religious schools, grain storage) are folded into the last
-- constructor; the Crown mining exception in (2A) is not encoded.
DECLARE `What my land is` IS ONE OF
    `a privately owned home`
    `a privately owned vacant lot`
    `Crown land used or held for a public purpose, or unoccupied`
    `land used exclusively as a place of public worship or a minister's residence`
    `land used exclusively as a nongovernment school`
    `land used exclusively for charitable purposes`
    `land exempt under another written law or declared exempt by the Minister`

GIVEN `my land` IS A `What my land is`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the land is rateable` IF
    CONSIDER `my land`
    WHEN `a privately owned home` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a privately owned vacant lot` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export Is my land rateable land (s 6.26)?
GIVEN `my land` IS A `What my land is` @desc What the land is and how it is used
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my land rateable` IF `the land is rateable` `my land`

-- s 6.53: land that becomes rateable during a financial year -- "the owner of that land (c) is liable for rates
-- proportionate to the portion of the year during which the land is rateable land; or (d) is entitled to a refund of an
-- amount proportionate to the portion of the year during which the land is not rateable land".
@export If my land became rateable during the year, how much of the year's rates must I pay (s 6.53)?
GIVEN `the rates for a full year` IS A NUMBER @desc The rates that would be payable for the whole financial year
      `days in the year the land is rateable` IS A NUMBER @desc Days of the financial year during which the land is rateable
      `days in the financial year` IS A NUMBER @desc 365, or 366 in a leap year
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much of the year's rates must I pay for land that became rateable` IS
    `the rates for a full year` TIMES `days in the year the land is rateable` DIVIDED BY `days in the financial year`

§§ `ss 6.32 and 6.44 -- who levies rates on my home, and who must pay them?`

-- s 6.32(1): "When adopting the annual budget, a local government -- (a) in order to make up the budget deficiency, is to
-- impose* a general rate on rateable land within its district ... (b) may impose* on rateable land within its district --
-- (i) a specified area rate; or (ii) a minimum payment; and (c) may impose* a service charge on land within its district."
@export Who levies rates on my home (s 6.32(1))?
GIVETH A STRING
DECIDE `Who levies rates on my home` IS
    "the local government of the district in which the land lies, when it adopts its annual budget (s 6.32(1))"

-- s 6.44(1): "The owner for the time being of land on which a rate or service charge has been imposed is liable to pay the
-- rate or service charge to the local government." (2): "If there are 2 or more owners of the land they are jointly and
-- severally liable". "Owner" (s 1.4, applied by s 6.25(b)) is in the first place "a person who is in possession as -- (i)
-- the holder of an estate of freehold in possession in the land" (a FACT here).
@export Am I liable to pay the rates on this land (s 6.44(1))?
GIVEN `I am the owner for the time being` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the owner of the land (s 1.4), for example the registered proprietor in possession
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I liable to pay the rates` IF `I am the owner for the time being`

@export Can I be made to pay all the rates on land I own with others (s 6.44(2))?
GIVEN `number of owners` IS A NUMBER @desc How many owners the land has, you included
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I be made to pay all the rates on land I own with others` IF `number of owners` AT LEAST 2

-- s 6.43: "Subject to the Rates and Charges (Rebates and Deferments) Act 1992, rates and service charges imposed under this
-- Act, together with the costs of proceedings, if any, for the recovery of the rates or service charges, are a charge on
-- the land rated".
@export Are unpaid rates a charge on my land (s 6.43)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Are unpaid rates a charge on my land` IS TRUE

-- s 6.55(1): rates "are recoverable by a local government from -- (i) the owner at the time of the compilation of the rate
-- record; or (ii) a person who whilst the rates or service charges are unpaid becomes the owner of the land."
@export Can the rates on this land be recovered from me (s 6.55(1))?
GIVEN `I was the owner when the rate record was compiled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you owned the land when the rate record for the year was compiled
      `I became the owner while the rates were unpaid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you became the owner while those rates were still unpaid
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the rates be recovered from me` IF
    `I was the owner when the rate record was compiled` OR `I became the owner while the rates were unpaid`

-- s 6.52(1)(a): rates "are apportionable between successive owners in respect of time as if they accrued due from day to
-- day during the period for which they were imposed". (2): an owner must reimburse another who paid a portion, "if there is
-- no agreement between them to the contrary". (3): "This section does not affect the liability of a person to the local
-- government."
@export As a buyer, what share of the year's rates is mine as between me and the seller (s 6.52(1)(a))?
GIVEN `the rates for the period` IS A NUMBER @desc The rates imposed for the period (usually the financial year)
      `days in the period` IS A NUMBER @desc Days in the period for which the rates were imposed
      `days I own the land in the period` IS A NUMBER @desc Days of that period from the day you became owner
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What share of the year's rates is mine as buyer` IS
    `the rates for the period` TIMES `days I own the land in the period` DIVIDED BY `days in the period`

@export Does apportionment with the seller change what the local government can recover from me (s 6.52(3))?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does apportionment change what the council can recover from me` IS FALSE

§§ `s 6.28 -- on what valuation is my land rated?`

-- s 6.28(1): "The Minister is to -- (a) determine the method of valuation of land to be used by a local government as the
-- basis for a rate; and (b) publish a notice of the determination in the Government Gazette."
-- (2): the Minister "is to have regard to the general principle that the basis for a rate on any land is to be -- (a) where
-- the land is used predominantly for rural purposes, the unimproved value of the land; and (b) where the land is used
-- predominantly for nonrural purposes, the gross rental value of the land."
-- The determination is the Minister's judgment: asked as a FACT, and where the owner does not know it, the answer is
-- conditional on the determination following the general principle. The values themselves are the Valuer-General's
-- under the Valuation of Land Act 1978 (s 6.25: "gross rental value" and "unimproved value" have the meaning given there).
DECLARE `Valuation basis` IS ONE OF
    `gross rental value`
    `unimproved value`
    `gross rental value, if the Minister's determination follows the general principle`
    `unimproved value, if the Minister's determination follows the general principle`

DECLARE `The Minister's determination for my land` IS ONE OF
    `gross rental value determined`
    `unimproved value determined`
    `not known to me`

@export On what valuation is my land rated, gross rental value or unimproved value (s 6.28(1), (2))?
GIVEN `the determination` IS A `The Minister's determination for my land` @desc The method of valuation the Minister has determined for your land, if you know it
      `used predominantly for rural purposes` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your land is used predominantly for rural purposes
GIVETH A `Valuation basis`
DECIDE `On what valuation is my land rated` IS
    CONSIDER `the determination`
    WHEN `gross rental value determined` THEN `gross rental value`
    WHEN `unimproved value determined` THEN `unimproved value`
    WHEN `not known to me` THEN
        IF `used predominantly for rural purposes`
        THEN `unimproved value, if the Minister's determination follows the general principle`
        ELSE `gross rental value, if the Minister's determination follows the general principle`

-- s 6.28(4): "the valuation to be used by a local government is to be the valuation in force under the Valuation of Land
-- Act 1978 as at 1 July in each financial year." (5): where during the year "(a) an interim valuation is made ...; or (b)
-- a valuation comes into force ... as a result of the amendment of a valuation ...; or (c) a new valuation is made ... in
-- the course of completing a general valuation", that valuation "is to be used".
DECLARE `Which valuation` IS ONE OF
    `the valuation in force on 1 July`
    `the interim, amended or new valuation made during the year`

@export Which valuation of my land are my rates worked out on this year (s 6.28(4), (5))?
GIVEN `an interim, amended or new valuation was made during the year` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, during the financial year, an interim valuation, an amended valuation or a new valuation in a general valuation came into force for your land
GIVETH A `Which valuation`
DECIDE `Which valuation are my rates worked out on` IS
    IF `an interim, amended or new valuation was made during the year`
    THEN `the interim, amended or new valuation made during the year`
    ELSE `the valuation in force on 1 July`

§§ `ss 6.32(2), 6.35 -- how much are my rates?`

-- s 6.32(2): a rate is "expressed as a rate in the dollar of the gross rental value" (or "of the unimproved value").
-- s 6.35(1): "a local government may impose on any rateable land in its district a minimum payment which is greater than
-- the general rate which would otherwise be payable on that land." So the owner pays the greater of the two. A minimum
-- payment of 0 means none was imposed.
GIVEN `the valuation` IS A NUMBER, `the rate in the dollar` IS A NUMBER, `the minimum payment` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the general rates on` MEANS
    `the greater of` (`the valuation` TIMES `the rate in the dollar`) `the minimum payment`

@export How much are my general rates, after any minimum payment (ss 6.32(2), 6.35(1))?
GIVEN `the valuation` IS A NUMBER @desc The gross rental value or unimproved value of your land in the rate record, in dollars
      `the rate in the dollar` IS A NUMBER @desc The general rate (or your differential rate) as a decimal, for example 0.07 for 7 cents in the dollar
      `the minimum payment` IS A NUMBER @desc The minimum payment the local government imposed for your rating category, or 0 if none
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much are my general rates` IS `the general rates on` `the valuation` `the rate in the dollar` `the minimum payment`

@export Am I paying the minimum payment rather than the rate in the dollar (s 6.35(1))?
GIVEN `the valuation` IS A NUMBER @desc The gross rental value or unimproved value of your land, in dollars
      `the rate in the dollar` IS A NUMBER @desc The general rate as a decimal
      `the minimum payment` IS A NUMBER @desc The minimum payment imposed, or 0 if none
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I paying the minimum payment` IF
    `the minimum payment` GREATER THAN (`the valuation` TIMES `the rate in the dollar`)

-- s 6.32(1)(b), (c): a specified area rate and a service charge are imposed in addition to the general rate.
@export What is the total on my rate notice for rates and service charges (s 6.32(1))?
GIVEN `the valuation` IS A NUMBER @desc The rateable value of your land, in dollars
      `the rate in the dollar` IS A NUMBER @desc The general rate as a decimal
      `the minimum payment` IS A NUMBER @desc The minimum payment imposed, or 0 if none
      `specified area rate` IS A NUMBER @desc Any specified area rate on your land, in dollars, or 0
      `service charges` IS A NUMBER @desc Any service charges on your land, in dollars, or 0
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the total of my rates and service charges` IS
    `the general rates on` `the valuation` `the rate in the dollar` `the minimum payment` PLUS `specified area rate` PLUS `service charges`

-- s 6.33(3): "a local government is not to, without the approval of the Minister, impose a differential general rate which
-- is more than twice the lowest differential general rate imposed by it."
@export Is my differential general rate within the limit of twice the lowest (s 6.33(3))?
GIVEN `my differential rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The differential general rate on your land, as a decimal
      `the lowest differential rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The lowest differential general rate the local government imposed
      `the Minister approved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Minister approved a higher rate
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my differential rate within the limit` IF
    `the Minister approved` OR `my differential rate` AT MOST (2 TIMES `the lowest differential rate`)

-- s 6.36(3)(b)(ii): the notice of proposed differential rates or minimum payments invites submissions "within 21 days (or
-- such longer period as is specified in the notice)".
@export How many days do I have to make a submission on proposed differential rates (s 6.36(3))?
GIVEN `days specified in the notice` IS A NUMBER @desc The period for submissions stated in the notice, in days, or 0 if none stated
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days do I have to make a submission` IS `the greater of` 21 `days specified in the notice`

§§ `ss 6.39-6.41, 6.33(4) -- my rate notice, and changes during the year`

-- s 6.41(1): the owner is to be given "a rate notice stating the date the rate notice was issued". (3): service "is deemed
-- to have been effected if delivered to the address shown in the rate record for the owner". (4): "The rate notice may be
-- given by email if the local government has obtained the consent of the owner".
@export Can my rate notice be sent to me by email (s 6.41(4))?
GIVEN `I consented to email` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you consented to receiving rate notices by email
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can my rate notice be emailed` IF `I consented to email`

@export Is my rate notice served if it was delivered to the address in the rate record, even if I did not get it (s 6.41(3))?
GIVEN `delivered to the address in the rate record` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was delivered to the address shown for you in the rate record
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my rate notice served` IF `delivered to the address in the rate record`

-- s 6.39(2)(a): the local government "is required, from time to time, to amend a rate record for the current financial
-- year to ensure that the information contained in the record is current and correct". s 6.40(1): where the record is
-- amended "as a result of a change in -- (a) the rateable value of; or (b) the rateability of; or (c) the rate imposed on,
-- that land, the local government is to reassess the rates payable on the land and to give notice to the owner".
-- s 6.33(4): if the characteristics on which a differential rate is based change during the year, the local government
-- "is not to, on account of that change, amend the assessment of rates payable on that land in respect of that financial
-- year but this subsection does not apply in any case where section 6.40(1)(a) applies."
@export Must my rates for this year be reassessed (ss 6.33(4), 6.40(1))?
GIVEN `the rateable value changed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the rateable value of your land changed during the year (for example an interim valuation after a house was built)
      `the rateability changed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your land became, or ceased to be, rateable
      `the rate imposed on it changed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the rate imposed on your land changed
      `only the land's differential rating characteristics changed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the only change was in the use, zoning or vacancy on which a differential rate is based
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my rates be reassessed` IF
        `the rateable value changed`
    OR  `the rateability changed`
    OR  (`the rate imposed on it changed` AND (NOT `only the land's differential rating characteristics changed`))

-- s 6.40(3)(a): if a reassessed rate is reduced and already paid, the local government "may, and if so requested by the
-- current owner of the land is required to, make a refund ... or (ii) is required to allow a credit".
DECLARE `What happens to an overpayment` IS ONE OF
    `a refund, because I asked for one`
    `a refund or a credit against future rates, as the local government chooses`
    `nothing: no overpayment`

@export If my rates are reassessed down after I paid, do I get a refund (s 6.40(3)(a))?
GIVEN `the amount paid` IS A NUMBER @desc What you have paid for the year
      `the reassessed rates` IS A NUMBER @desc The rates for the year after reassessment
      `I asked for a refund` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (the current owner) asked for a refund
GIVETH A `What happens to an overpayment`
DECIDE `Do I get a refund of overpaid rates` IS
    IF `the amount paid` GREATER THAN `the reassessed rates`
    THEN IF `I asked for a refund`
         THEN `a refund, because I asked for one`
         ELSE `a refund or a credit against future rates, as the local government chooses`
    ELSE `nothing: no overpayment`

§§ `ss 6.45, 6.50 -- when are my rates due, and can I pay by instalments?`

-- s 6.50(2): "The date determined by a local government under subsection (1) is not to be earlier than 35 days after the
-- date noted on the rate notice as the date the rate notice was issued." (Subject, by (1), to concessions and the Rates and
-- Charges (Rebates and Deferments) Act 1992.)
@export What is the fewest days after my rate notice was issued that my rates can fall due (s 6.50(2))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the fewest days after the notice that my rates can fall due` IS 35

@export Is the due date on my rate notice lawful (s 6.50(2))?
GIVEN `the issue date` IS A NUMBER @desc The date the notice says it was issued, as YYYYMMDD
      `the due date` IS A NUMBER @desc The due date on the notice (or of the first instalment), as YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my due date lawful` IF `the days from` `the issue date` `the due date` AT LEAST 35

-- s 6.45(1): "A rate or service charge is ordinarily payable to a local government by a single payment but the person
-- liable ... may elect to make that payment ... by -- (a) 4 equal or nearly equal instalments; or (b) such other method of
-- payment by instalments as is set forth in the local government's annual budget."
-- (2): after a reassessment under s 6.40, the person may pay "(a) by a single payment; or (b) by such instalments as are
-- remaining ... for the remainder of that financial year."
DECLARE `How I may pay` IS ONE OF
    `a single payment, or 4 instalments, or another instalment plan in the budget`
    `a single payment, or the instalments remaining this year`

@export How may I pay my rates (s 6.45(1), (2))?
GIVEN `this notice follows a reassessment during the year` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this rate notice was given after your rates were reassessed during the year
GIVETH A `How I may pay`
DECIDE `How may I pay my rates` IS
    IF `this notice follows a reassessment during the year`
    THEN `a single payment, or the instalments remaining this year`
    ELSE `a single payment, or 4 instalments, or another instalment plan in the budget`

-- s 6.45(3): "A local government may impose an additional charge (including an amount by way of interest) where payment ...
-- is made by instalments", up to a prescribed maximum (s 6.45(4)(e), not deposited: an INPUT). s 6.51(4)(b): "no
-- additional charge is to be imposed under section 6.45(3)" on a person entitled to a rebate or deferment.
@export How much is each of my 4 instalments, with any instalment charge (s 6.45(1)(a), (3))?
GIVEN `the rates and service charges` IS A NUMBER @desc The total on your rate notice
      `the instalment charge` IS A NUMBER @desc The additional charge the local government imposes for paying by instalments, in total
      `I am entitled to a rebate or deferment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are entitled to a rebate or deferment under the Rates and Charges (Rebates and Deferments) Act 1992 (or one the local government resolved to give)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much is each instalment` IS
    IF `I am entitled to a rebate or deferment`
    THEN `the rates and service charges` DIVIDED BY 4
    ELSE (`the rates and service charges` PLUS `the instalment charge`) DIVIDED BY 4

-- s 6.50(3): "the second and each subsequent instalment does not become due and payable at intervals of less than 2
-- months." s 6.41(2)(b): the rate notice for an instalment is to be given "not less than 28 days before each instalment is
-- due".
@export Is this instalment due date lawful (s 6.50(3))?
GIVEN `the previous instalment's due date` IS A NUMBER @desc The due date of the previous instalment, as YYYYMMDD
      `this instalment's due date` IS A NUMBER @desc The due date of this instalment, as YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this instalment due date lawful` IF
    `this instalment's due date` AT LEAST `two months after` `the previous instalment's due date`

@export Was I given my instalment notice early enough (s 6.41(2)(b))?
GIVEN `the day the notice was given` IS A NUMBER @desc The day the instalment notice was given to you, as YYYYMMDD
      `the instalment's due date` IS A NUMBER @desc The due date of the instalment, as YYYYMMDD
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was my instalment notice early enough` IF
    `the days from` `the day the notice was given` `the instalment's due date` AT LEAST 28

-- s 6.46: the local government "may, when imposing a rate or service charge, resolve* to grant a discount or other incentive
-- for the early payment". s 6.12(2): the general discount power in s 6.12(1)(a), (b) does "not apply to an amount of money
-- owing in respect of rates and service charges".
@export Do I get a discount for paying my rates early (s 6.46)?
GIVEN `the local government resolved a discount when imposing the rates` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the council resolved, when imposing the rates, to give a discount or incentive for early payment
      `I paid by the discount date` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you paid in time to meet the terms of that discount
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I get an early payment discount` IF
    `the local government resolved a discount when imposing the rates` AND `I paid by the discount date`

§§ `s 6.51 -- interest on late rates, and the rebates connection`

-- s 6.51(1): the local government "may at the time of imposing a rate or service charge resolve* to impose interest (at
-- the rate set in its annual budget) on -- (a) a rate or service charge (or any instalment ...); and (b) any costs of
-- proceedings ... that remains unpaid after becoming due and payable."
-- (4): "If a person is entitled under the Rates and Charges (Rebates and Deferments) Act 1992 or under this Act (if the
-- local government in a particular case so resolves) to a rebate or deferment in respect of a rate or service charge --
-- (a) no interest is to accrue in respect of that rate or service charge payable by that person".
@export Does interest run on my overdue rates (s 6.51(1), (4))?
GIVEN `the local government resolved to charge interest when imposing the rates` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the council resolved, when imposing the rates, to charge interest on overdue amounts
      `the rates are unpaid after the due date` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the rates (or an instalment) remain unpaid after becoming due
      `I am entitled to a rebate or deferment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are entitled to a rebate or deferment under the Rates and Charges (Rebates and Deferments) Act 1992 (or one the local government resolved to give)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does interest run on my overdue rates` IF
        `the local government resolved to charge interest when imposing the rates`
    AND `the rates are unpaid after the due date`
    AND (NOT `I am entitled to a rebate or deferment`)

-- s 6.51(2): the rate "is not to exceed the rate for the time being prescribed as the maximum rate of interest" (the
-- prescribed maximum is set by regulations not deposited: an INPUT).
@export What is the highest rate of interest I can be charged on overdue rates, % a year (s 6.51(1), (2))?
GIVEN `the rate in the budget` IS A NUMBER @desc The interest rate set in the local government's annual budget, % a year
      `the prescribed maximum` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum rate prescribed by regulations, % a year
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the highest interest rate I can be charged` IS `the lesser of` `the rate in the budget` `the prescribed maximum`

-- s 6.51(5): "Regulations may provide for the method of calculation of interest." Those regulations are not deposited.
-- ASSUMPTION: simple interest, by the day, on the amount overdue, from the due date to the day of payment, on a 365-day
-- year. A day count, not a statement of the prescribed method.
@export About how much interest will I be charged on overdue rates (s 6.51, simple daily interest assumed)?
GIVEN `the amount overdue` IS A NUMBER @desc The rates or instalment unpaid after the due date, in dollars
      `the rate in the budget` IS A NUMBER @desc The interest rate set in the budget, % a year
      `the prescribed maximum` IS A NUMBER @desc The prescribed maximum rate, % a year
      `the due date` IS A NUMBER @desc The due date, as YYYYMMDD
      `the day I pay` IS A NUMBER @desc The day you pay, as YYYYMMDD
      `the local government resolved to charge interest when imposing the rates` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the council resolved to charge interest
      `I am entitled to a rebate or deferment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are entitled to a rebate or deferment under the Rates and Charges (Rebates and Deferments) Act 1992
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `About how much interest will I be charged` IS
    IF      `the local government resolved to charge interest when imposing the rates`
        AND (NOT `I am entitled to a rebate or deferment`)
        AND `days late` GREATER THAN 0
    THEN `the amount overdue` TIMES (`the lesser of` `the rate in the budget` `the prescribed maximum`) DIVIDED BY 100 TIMES `days late` DIVIDED BY 365
    ELSE 0
    WHERE
        `days late` MEANS `the days from` `the due date` `the day I pay`

@export Where do I find the pensioner and seniors rebates and deferments of rates?
GIVETH A STRING
DECIDE `Where are the pensioner and seniors rebates and deferments` IS
    "the Rates and Charges (Rebates and Deferments) Act 1992 (WA); this Act defers to it in ss 6.43, 6.46, 6.47, 6.50(1)(c), 6.51(4) and 6.55(1)"

§§ `Subdivision 5 -- recovery of unpaid rates`

-- s 6.56(1): "If a rate or service charge remains unpaid after it becomes due and payable, the local government may recover
-- it, as well as the costs of proceedings, if any, for that recovery, in a court of competent jurisdiction."
@export Can the local government sue me for my rates (s 6.56(1))?
GIVEN `the rates are unpaid after the due date` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the rates remain unpaid after becoming due
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the local government sue me for my rates` IF `the rates are unpaid after the due date`

-- s 6.62: money paid is applied "(a) [to] the rates or services charges due on the land in the order in which they become
-- due; and (b) [to] any outstanding costs of proceedings".
@export Is my payment applied to my oldest rates first (s 6.62)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my payment applied to my oldest rates first` IS TRUE

-- s 6.60(2): if rates are due and payable, "notice may be given to the lessee of the land requiring the lessee to pay to
-- the local government any rent as it falls due in satisfaction of the rate". (5)(b): where the lessor is liable for the
-- rate, "the amount paid may be set off by the lessee against the rent payable to the lessor".
@export As a tenant, must I pay my rent to the local government instead of my landlord (s 6.60)?
GIVEN `the landlord's rates are due and payable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether rates on the land you lease are due and unpaid
      `I was given a notice under s 6.60` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government gave you notice to pay your rent to it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I pay my rent to the local government` IF
    `the landlord's rates are due and payable` AND `I was given a notice under s 6.60`

-- s 6.61(1)(a): "the occupier of property, or an agent of the owner of property, is required to disclose to the local
-- government the name and address of the owner". (2): failing to, or giving false or misleading information, is an offence.
-- "Penalty for this subsection: a fine of $5 000."
@export What is the most I can be fined for not telling the local government who owns the property I occupy (s 6.61)?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined for not naming the owner` IS 5000

§§ `Subdivision 6 -- what can be done to my land if rates stay unpaid`

-- s 6.64(1): "If any rates or service charges which are due to a local government in respect of any rateable land have been
-- unpaid for at least 3 years the local government may ... take possession of the land ... and -- (a) ... lease the land;
-- or (b) sell the land; or (c) cause the land to be transferred to the Crown; or (d) cause the land to be transferred to
-- itself." (3): "Where payment of rates ... is in arrears the local government has an interest in the land in respect of
-- which it may lodge a caveat".
@export Can the local government take possession of my land for unpaid rates (s 6.64(1))?
GIVEN `years the oldest unpaid rates have been unpaid` IS A NUMBER @desc How long the oldest unpaid rates have been unpaid, in years
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the local government take possession of my land` IF
    `years the oldest unpaid rates have been unpaid` AT LEAST 3

@export Can the local government lodge a caveat over my land for unpaid rates (s 6.64(3))?
GIVEN `my rates are in arrears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any rates on the land are in arrears
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the local government lodge a caveat over my land` IF `my rates are in arrears`

-- s 6.67(1): "Where, within 12 years of the taking of possession ... an entitled person pays ... all rates and service
-- charges due and payable ... the local government is required to give up possession ... unless it has exercised a power
-- under section 6.64(1)(b), (c) or (d)".
@export If I pay all the arrears, must the local government give my land back (s 6.67(1))?
GIVEN `years since possession was taken` IS A NUMBER @desc How long ago the local government took possession, in years
      `the land has been sold or transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it has already sold the land or transferred it to the Crown or itself
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the local government give my land back` IF
    `years since possession was taken` AT MOST 12 AND (NOT `the land has been sold or transferred`)

-- s 6.69(1): "Up to 7 days prior to the time of the actual sale ... a person having an estate or interest in the land may
-- pay the rates or service charges and the costs and expenses incurred". (2): after that, the local government "may, upon
-- such terms and conditions as are agreed ... accept payment". (3): on payment "the proceedings relating to the proposed
-- sale are stayed".
DECLARE `Can I stop the sale` IS ONE OF
    `yes, by paying the rates and the costs so far`
    `only if the local government agrees to accept payment`
    `no: the sale has happened`

@export Can I stop the sale of my land by paying the arrears (s 6.69)?
GIVEN `days before the sale` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days before the time of the sale you would pay (0 or less if the sale has happened)
GIVETH A `Can I stop the sale`
DECIDE `Can I stop the sale by paying` IS
    IF `days before the sale` AT LEAST 7
    THEN `yes, by paying the rates and the costs so far`
    ELSE IF `days before the sale` GREATER THAN 0
         THEN `only if the local government agrees to accept payment`
         ELSE `no: the sale has happened`

§§ `Subdivision 7 -- objecting to my rates`

-- s 6.76(1): "A person may ... object to the rate record ... on the ground -- (a) that there is an error in the rate record
-- -- (i) with respect to the identity of the owner or occupier of any land; or (ii) on the basis that the land or part of
-- the land is not rateable land; or (b) if the local government imposes a differential general rate, that the
-- characteristics of the land recorded in the rate record as the basis for imposing that rate should be deleted and other
-- characteristics substituted."
-- s 6.80: "There is not to be an objection or review in respect of a valuation of rateable land appearing in a rate record
-- except in accordance with the Valuation of Land Act 1978."
-- s 6.82(1): "Where there is a question of general interest as to whether a rate or service charge was imposed in
-- accordance with this Act, the local government or any person may refer the question to the State Administrative
-- Tribunal"; (2) not for "a question relating to that person's own individual case" that could be resolved under s 6.76.
DECLARE `My complaint` IS ONE OF
    `the rate record names the wrong owner or occupier`
    `my land, or part of it, is not rateable`
    `the rate record gives my land the wrong rating category`
    `my valuation is too high`
    `the rate was not imposed in accordance with the Act`
    `the rate in the dollar is too high`

DECLARE `Where my complaint goes` IS ONE OF
    `an objection to the local government under s 6.76`
    `an objection under the Valuation of Land Act 1978, not this Act (s 6.80)`
    `a reference of a question of general interest to the State Administrative Tribunal (s 6.82)`
    `no objection lies; a submission when rates are proposed (s 6.36)`

@export Where does my complaint about my rates go (ss 6.76, 6.80, 6.82)?
GIVEN `my complaint` IS A `My complaint` @desc What you say is wrong
      `the local government imposes differential general rates` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government imposes differential general rates
GIVETH A `Where my complaint goes`
DECIDE `Where does my complaint go` IS
    CONSIDER `my complaint`
    WHEN `the rate record names the wrong owner or occupier` THEN `an objection to the local government under s 6.76`
    WHEN `my land, or part of it, is not rateable` THEN `an objection to the local government under s 6.76`
    WHEN `the rate record gives my land the wrong rating category` THEN
        IF `the local government imposes differential general rates`
        THEN `an objection to the local government under s 6.76`
        ELSE `no objection lies; a submission when rates are proposed (s 6.36)`
    WHEN `my valuation is too high` THEN `an objection under the Valuation of Land Act 1978, not this Act (s 6.80)`
    WHEN `the rate was not imposed in accordance with the Act` THEN `a reference of a question of general interest to the State Administrative Tribunal (s 6.82)`
    WHEN `the rate in the dollar is too high` THEN `no objection lies; a submission when rates are proposed (s 6.36)`

-- s 6.76(2): the objection is to "(a) be made to the local government in writing within 42 days of the service of a rate
-- notice under section 6.41; and (b) identify the relevant land; and (c) set out fully and in detail the grounds". (3): it
-- "may be made by the person named in the rate record as the owner of land or by the agent or attorney of that person". (4):
-- the local government "may, on application ... extend the time".
@export Is my objection to the rate record validly made (s 6.76(2)-(4))?
GIVEN `days since the rate notice was served` IS A NUMBER @desc Days from service of the rate notice to the day you lodge the objection
      `the local government extended the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the local government extended the time for you
      `in writing, identifying the land, with full and detailed grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the objection is in writing, identifies the land and sets out the grounds fully and in detail
      `I am the owner named in the rate record, or their agent or attorney` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the owner named in the rate record, or that owner's agent or attorney
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my objection validly made` IF
        (`days since the rate notice was served` AT MOST 42 OR `the local government extended the time`)
    AND `in writing, identifying the land, with full and detailed grounds`
    AND `I am the owner named in the rate record, or their agent or attorney`

-- s 6.77: a person dissatisfied with the decision on the objection "may, within 42 days (or such further period as the
-- State Administrative Tribunal, for reasonable cause shown by the person, allows) after service of notice of the decision,
-- apply to the State Administrative Tribunal for a review". s 6.78: a refusal to extend time is also reviewable.
@export Am I in time to ask the State Administrative Tribunal to review the decision on my objection (s 6.77)?
GIVEN `days since notice of the decision was served` IS A NUMBER @desc Days from service of the notice of the decision on your objection
      `the Tribunal allowed a further period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the State Administrative Tribunal allowed you more time for reasonable cause
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I in time to apply for a review` IF
    `days since notice of the decision was served` AT MOST 42 OR `the Tribunal allowed a further period`

-- s 6.81: "The making of an objection under this Subdivision does not affect the liability to pay any rate or service
-- charge imposed under this Act pending determination of the objection."
@export Must I still pay my rates while my objection is decided (s 6.81)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I still pay while my objection is decided` IS TRUE
